  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "A tide graph is a smooth wave. High tide reaches a depth of $8$ ft and low tide drops to $2$ ft. What is the amplitude of the wave?",
  "answerType": "multiple-choice",
  "choices": [
    {
      "id": "A",
      "label": "$3$ ft",
      "correct": true
    },
    {
      "id": "B",
      "label": "$5$ ft",
      "correct": false
    },
    {
      "id": "C",
      "label": "$6$ ft",
      "correct": false
    },
    {
      "id": "D",
      "label": "$8$ ft",
      "correct": false
    }
  ],
  "primaryAnswer": "A",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Amplitude is the distance from the MIDLINE to a peak, not from the bottom to the top.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Amplitude is the distance from the MIDLINE to a peak, not from the bottom to the top."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Amplitude $= \\frac{\\text{max} - \\text{min}}{2} = \\frac{8 - 2}{2}$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$\\frac{6}{2} = 3$ ft."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "The full swing from low tide to high tide is $8 - 2 = 6$ ft.",
    "The amplitude is only HALF of that swing, since it measures from the midline up to the peak.",
    "So the amplitude is $\\frac{6}{2} = 3$ ft (and the midline is at $5$ ft)."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Find the midline first",
      "steps": [
        "The midline sits halfway between the extremes: $\\frac{8 + 2}{2} = 5$ ft.",
        "From $5$ ft up to high tide at $8$ ft is 3 ft, and from $5$ ft down to low tide at $2$ ft is also 3 ft.",
        "That matching distance is the amplitude: 3 ft. (Choice B, 5 ft, is the midline; choice C, 6 ft, is the full range.)"
      ]
    }
  ],
